  "final_message": "The bug was on line 10 of `session_ttl.py`: it removed the timezone from `now` but not from `created`. Subtracting a timezone-aware time from one without a timezone raised `TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`. I removed the `.replace(tzinfo=None)` call, so both timestamps are now parsed the same way. `format_expiry_message` is unchanged.\n\n`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes (1/1). I also tried three extra cases by hand, all correct:\n- **30s elapsed, 60s TTL:** not expired.\n- **Timestamps without a timezone:** 60s elapsed, 60s TTL, expired.\n- **Mixed offsets:** `+00:00` against `+01:00`, one hour elapsed, one-hour TTL, expired.\n\nA timezone-aware timestamp compared with one without a timezone will still raise that `TypeError`, because the README only covers timestamps that include a timezone. The formatter warning for this file comes from line 7, which I didn't touch.\n\nDONE: Fixed `session_ttl_expired` by no longer removing the timezone from `now` alone, so timezone-aware timestamps compare correctly; test_public.py passes.",
